  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers are expected to impact portions of the \nregion through tonight.\n\nLow pressure developing along the stalled frontal boundary to\nour south early this morning will provide another round of \nshowers across portions of the region. The timing of the showers\nwill be focused during the afternoon hours Sunday, but could \nbegin near/south of Philly in the morning and linger area wide \ninto the evening or overnight hours. PoPs have retreated \nslightly and now range from around 30-50% along and north of \nI-80 to around 45-55% within the I-95 corridor and Philly \nmetro, to 60-70% across Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. \nThe latest QPF forecast has around 0.10\" or less along the I-95 \ncorridor and points north and west with around 0.25-0.75\" across\nthe southern Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. It's possible\nthe showers don't make it much farther northwest than the I-95 \ncorridor. There still remains some spread in the guidance as to \nexactly how far north and west the showers will make it. \n\nGiven overcast skies, easterly winds, and showers, temperatures\ntoday will be cooler with highs likely only reaching the low to\nmid 60s in many areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Canadian high pressure will bring much cooler \ntemperatures and dry weather through much of the week.\n\nCanadian high pressure and a much cooler air mass will build into \nthe region starting Monday. Monday will feature mostly sunny skies\nwith highs within a few degrees of 70, though a stiff northwest\nbreeze will make it feel cooler. Sunny skies continue on Tuesday,\nthough highs will only be in the upper 50s to mid 60s for much \nof the region which is upwards of 10 degrees below normal. Lows \nwill fall mainly into the 40s, with 50s along the coast and the \nupper 30s in the Poconos. Some areas of frost will be possible \nmainly north of I-78 late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning.\n\nAs cold advection wanes by mid-week, the airmass will begin to \nmodify and trend warmer as southerly flow returns. Highs remain in \nthe 60s on Wednesday, before warming back into the 70s again for \nThursday and Friday. With high pressure remaining in control, mostly \nclear skies and dry weather is expected through the end of the\nweek.",
